WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.880
Officially, this suspension bridge
over the River Lee, in Cork,

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.120
in the south of Ireland,
is called "Daly's Bridge",

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.760
after a businessman who helped
fund it back in the 1920s.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.320
But unofficially, it has a different name:
the Shakey Bridge.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.160
Because it shakes.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.920
I was going to film this
with my GoPro on a stick walking across,

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.160
but it's a really narrow bridge,

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.120
and I don't want to end up barging
into the people who are actually using it.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.800
I can confirm from a
brief experiment, though...

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.840
Yep. It's bouncy!

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.480
- In the early 1700s, there was a ferry boat
that actually brought people from here

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.680
across to the other side.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:39.640
But unfortunately between 1906 and
1908, the Dooley family actually retired,

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.920
retired themselves
from the ferry boat industry.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.760
And it was through the philanthropy
of a man called James Daly, who gave

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.480
nearly £700 towards the construction
of a bridge project.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.840
£700 in today's terms would be
around €50,000.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.120
And to be honest,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.800
you would not be able to build a bridge
in today's world for €50,000.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.440
So the bridge was good value.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.560
David Rowell and Company,
in London, Westminster,

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.400
actually had a bridge catalogue.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.960
And the Corporation of Cork
bought a bridge off the catalogue,

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.800
and in early 1927 these huge,
giant wooden boxes

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.920
arrived on the banks of the River Lee.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.960
And the bridge actually was constructed.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.200
So in the first couple of months
the people of the area

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.640
discovered that the bridge actually shook
when they walked over it.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.240
- Now, all suspension bridges,
actually, pretty much all bridges,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.360
will move or shake
or sway a little

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.800
as the dynamic loads on them
from people and weather change.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.640
And that's a good thing: because
if they don't move a little...

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.600
...they break.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.200
The design of this bridge
meant the shake was more obvious.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.480
It bounces up and down.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.640
And it probably wasn't intentional(!)
But clearly it wasn't dangerous, as proven

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.040
by the fact that the bridge
stood for 90 years,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.760
taking all the jumping around
and abuse

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.920
that local children, and adults,
could throw at it.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.680
But nothing lasts forever.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.960
In 2017, an inspection
looked at the corrosion and damage

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.080
that had accumulated
over the years on the bridge.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.560
And, well, it'd been built to last
with good materials.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.600
But we're near the ocean here,
and 90 years of salty air and shaking

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.000
meant that the metal had corroded,
and wires had broken and frayed.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.520
The report said, in short:
the bridge had about three years

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:16.400
before it had to be closed,
and repairs were needed immediately.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.880
So it was repaired.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.720
Not rebuilt: the engineers reused
or matched materials as far as possible.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.360
They took the deck of the bridge apart, fixed
it up off-site, and then put it back together,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.080
taking care to keep the height
and positioning the same.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.080
They replaced the suspension cables
with brand new ones,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.680
and repaired the towers where they stood.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.680
And they gave the bridge
a fresh coat of paint.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:34.960
But here's the problem.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.520
This bridge is on the local Record
of Protected Structures.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.240
It must be maintained and repaired,
but it also can't be changed.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.480
- There was public consultation, and
of course many people actually wrote in,

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.400
going "keep the shake, keep the shake,
keep the shake, keep the shake".

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.920
And in fairness to the engineers
who worked on the project,

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.360
I think they did achieve that.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:53.480
People have had a lot of fun

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.320
walking across the bridge,
admiring the river, jumping on it.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.800
Like, this has been ongoing for 90 years.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.120
- Before the work started,
the engineers used accelerometers

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.120
to measure how the bridge responded
to someone jumping right in the middle.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.640
And they found that the bridge bounces
up and down at about 2.3 cycles a second.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.840
So as part of the design process,
they used computer modelling

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.280
to work out how the
new cables would behave.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.240
And they made sure to keep the bridge's
total mass as close as possible

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.720
to how it was before.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.400
Bridges shake and twist
in three dimensions, of course,

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.560
it's not just up-and-down.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.520
But up-and-down is the direction

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.440
you feel the most
when you're standing up there!

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.280
And all the data showed that,
in all three dimensions, the repaired

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.720
the repaired Shakey Bridge was
moving more or less as it did before.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:34.640
There was a slight change.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:35.880
But arguably that just took it

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.920
closer to how it shook when it was
brand new, nearly a century earlier.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:42.680
The bridge now goes up and down
at 2.2 cycles a second.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.360
It would have been entirely possible,
with modern technology and materials,

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.800
to build an identical looking bridge
that was much more stable.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.160
Normally, that's what engineers do:
they try to damp down vibrations.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:55.080
But in this one case,
their job was to keep things the same.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.440
Fixing the shake
would not have been preservation.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.000
The goal here was to repair this bridge
so that it can survive,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.160
and shake the residents of Cork
for another century to come.

